artifacts:
  - $route/.claims/3.verify.yield.md

reviews:
  self:
    - slug: has-verified-and-sorted-by-permanence
      say: |
        did the verify stone confirm each claim and sort by the permanence trigger?

        BLOCKERS:
        - a claim marked CLOSED/confirmed with no command + output behind it (a claim, not a confirmation)
        - a REDETECT-ABSENT key from detect §2.0 left un-flipped (not moved to CLOSED — the promote convergence test waits on that flip), OR flipped to CLOSED with no detect re-run behind it
        - a false positive kept as a work item (a placeholder named a secret, a carve-out named a leak)
        - the sort is by SEVERITY, not by the permanence trigger (leaks by deadline, infil+exfil by blast radius)
        - leaks and infil/exfil interleaved before the end (the cheap irreversible leaks must not expire behind attacker-triggered items)
        - a third-party disclosure routed to a repair or an issue instead of a private channel
        - a disposition absent on any verified claim
        - the roadmap written anywhere but the gitignored `.claims/` path (it names live claims — a driven + committed route would publish the map on the next push)
        - the roadmap emitted with no not-security-advice disclaimer (it is a recommendation surface a human acts on — rule.require.recommendation-disclaimer)

        a peer read is a lead, never a verdict. the sort key follows who fires the permanence, not how scary it sounds.
